  "name": "Question 1",
  "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14066449/assets/c06da8f06e8d640c23dbd81885fdd1bd5e5d89b15b1729cacd82ac52f301e44d.png)\n\nFind the area bounded by the polar curve $r={e}^{3\\theta }$ for $0\\le \\theta \\le 2\\pi ,$ as shown above.",
  "worked_solution": "The area bounded by a polar curve is given by\n\n$$\nA=\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}{r}^{2}(\\theta )\\text{d}\\theta \\,.\n$$\n\nWhen $r(\\theta )={e}^{3\\theta }$, ${\\theta }_{1}=0$ and ${\\theta }_{2}=2\\pi $, this gives \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}{r}^{2}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{({e}^{3\\theta })}^{2}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{e}^{6\\theta }\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot \\frac{1}{6}{e}^{6\\theta }{|}_{0}^{2\\pi } \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{12}({e}^{12\\pi }-{e}^{0}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{12}({e}^{12\\pi }-1).\\end{aligned}\n$$",
